WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.767
Hi, I'm Oriel!

00:00:02.767 --> 00:00:07.698
Apple's new benchmark asks whether coding agents can improve the engines that serve models.

00:00:07.698 --> 00:00:10.515
Here's how one task is built and graded.

00:00:12.017 --> 00:00:15.629
The idea: seal a real engine, let the agent work offline, then

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:18.639
build and serve what it left behind, and measure it.

00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:25.699
Each task starts from a real engine tree at one commit, with

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:28.498
its git history, version stamp and file dates removed.

00:00:28.918 --> 00:00:32.528
The agent gets that tree, a model and up to eight GPUs,

00:00:32.528 --> 00:00:34.634
and works offline for about eight hours.

00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:38.738
A separate verifier builds the edited tree and the untouched one on

00:00:38.738 --> 00:00:40.580
the same machine, and serves both.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.457
Day-zero support is pass or fail on hidden checks.

00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:47.531
Optimization and bug fixes score the measured speed-up.

00:00:49.025 --> 00:00:52.241
A speed-up is a ratio, so the baseline has to be honest.

00:00:52.661 --> 00:00:55.061
First, both trees decode the same prompts.

00:00:55.061 --> 00:00:56.433
Any difference scores zero.

00:00:56.853 --> 00:00:58.336
Warmup runs are thrown away.

00:00:58.336 --> 00:01:01.006
A cold first run faked a ten percent gain.

00:01:01.426 --> 00:01:05.334
Rounds rotate which tree goes first, so neither side carries the slow start.

00:01:05.754 --> 00:01:09.373
Each shape takes the median of paired ratios, then the shapes are averaged.

00:01:09.793 --> 00:01:12.615
And a guard shape must not get slower, or the run fails.

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:18.271
The headline, twenty-six point seven percent, is one track: the best agent

00:01:18.271 --> 00:01:20.030
on the ten day-zero tasks.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.140
Half of those tasks nobody passed.

00:01:23.842 --> 00:01:25.713
On the speed-up tasks it flips.

00:01:25.713 --> 00:01:29.454
Nine of twelve agents beat the maintainer's own fix on this regression.

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:34.077
Landing a new model is still a wall.

00:01:34.077 --> 00:01:37.541
Making a served model faster already goes past the human fix.

00:01:39.983 --> 00:01:43.572
A sealed engine, honest benches, and two very different answers.

00:01:44.583 --> 00:01:46.631
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:47.216
I'm Oriel.

00:01:47.216 --> 00:01:47.509
Bye!
